WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.180
Good morning, John. Two days from now will be the tenth anniversary of the first vlogbrothers video being uploaded to YouTube.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.200
There were people who watched that video when they were eleven year olds who can now get drunk.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.400
In those ten years I have done a lot of things that dreams are made of.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:19.160
I made so many amazing friends; I toured in a rock band; I played Carnegie Hall; I bought my first home; I grew up, stayed young, created VidCon

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.780
and SciShow and Crash Course;

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.060
helped other amazing, talented creators turn their visions into real things;

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.680
I became a bobble head, invented 2D glasses, and I interviewed the President.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.620
But of all the things we've done in the last ten years, the thing that I am most proud of

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:31.800
is this community.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.200
And I know that that sounds hokey and made up, but let me explain.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.200
We are in a time of cultural upheaval.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.160
Now that's probably true of everyone who's ever lived but it seems particularly strong right now.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.940
The internet is gonna have an unknowable effect on human culture,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.640
and I think that effect is gonna be huge and it's gonna be both positive and negative.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.280
The thing that's been occupying my mind a lot lately, and this isn't just the internet,

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.020
this has been going on for a long time,

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.680
is the disappearance of traditional structures for community,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.060
and that, so far, not a lot has taken the place of those structures

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.020
except for just diehard individualism.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.020
And there are good things that come from that

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:12.340
but I think it's also sort of resulted in an explosion of distrust among people

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.460
and an explosion of cynicism, especially in young people.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.500
I don't like the idea that the only thing you can put your faith in is yourself because

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.880
one, it's not true, and two, where does it leave us, in those moments that we all have

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.280
when we don't actually like ourselves that much?

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.620
And those moments might be getting more common as we see

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.500
all of these people on the internet with their perfect lives and their pretty faces

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.520
and their giant travel budgets and all their followers.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.380
I don't want individualism to go away, nor do I think I could make that happen,

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.680
But it's so new, and so different, and so pervasive that there aren't a lot of structures

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.580
to help it exist while also not destroying society.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.020
Now that's a big statement that's based on not a lot of research

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:54.660
But, I don't think it's controversial to say that we need new community structures.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.220
Now a lot of people would and have said that John and I, we are leaders of a community,

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.540
and of course in many ways that is true,

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.760
but I honestly believe that, more than being a leader of this community, I am a follower of it.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.940
So for ten years, we've tried to build a place where people can be comfortable and excited

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.480
and enthusiastic in a tumultuous and weird moment in history.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:19.380
But we have not done that by seeing this target ten years off and charging straight through the forest at it.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.120
We didn't know what we were doing, we didn't know how to do it.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.720
I do what I am being told to do.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.600
When people leave comments and they say this was my favorite video ever.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.920
When people say, "Thank you, this really helped me," or, "I feel like a better person after watching this video,"

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.800
that's how we choose what we do.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.800
I am being lead through this process. I don't know what I'm doing.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.820
But somehow, over these ten years, by wanting to do this, and by listening,

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:46.920
we've created a community that provides joy and connection and value and even meaning

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.040
for a lot of people, definitely for me,

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.680
that doesn't look like any of the previous things that were a community.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:57.560
I've got to go back and say that individualism is great like we need it, we need to believe in ourselves.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:02.880
We need to give that power to individual people, but we can't also discard community.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.160
Striking that balance is a huge challenge that we need to face right now,

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.300
because there are big problems that need to be faced, not by these leaderless collectives that

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.620
we have seen, where the individuals have tons of energy but there's no

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.980
cohesive community to focus that energy.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:21.840
We need to figure out how to be both leaders and followers.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.800
How to be both ourselves and part of something bigger than ourselves.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.520
We've spent ten years trying to figure out how to do that. It's hard. It's complicated.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.200
The model doesn't exist.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:39.040
And this is why I say that Nerdfighteria is the most interesting thing that I have ever been a part of.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.860
It is a model for that.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.620
We have had the time to try the things out.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.660
We figured out some things that worked, some things that haven't,

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:51.160
and other people elsewhere have also figured those things out, and they also lead us.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:57.800
People from Mark Twain to Vi Hart, from Michelle Obama to Ze Frank, from Reinhold Niebuhr to Nathan Zed.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.840
Like wide varieties of amazing people.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.200
I really think we need to find the tools for how to do this and we need to find them pretty quick.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.340
So I look underneath the ten years of the amazing things that we've done,

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:14.040
like things that are in the news, and people talked about and like, people respect us for.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:20.780
But underneath all of it is this thing that's confusing and hard to understand and amorphous that is this community.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:27.640
That no one understands how big of a deal it is, how interesting it is, and how, maybe, important it is.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:31.480
So I say that Nerdfighteria is the thing that I'm most proud of,

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.720
of all the very cool things that I've been able to do.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:40.080
I'm not saying that as some kind of like, you know, slogan to try and like, increase enthusiasm for the community,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.560
though, absolutely I want that.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:47.480
I'm not saying that just as like a cheerleader for the values of this community, though I am also one of those.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:05:00.880
I'm saying it because it's a model for how we build community in a distributed, internet-based, hyper individualized world,

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.660
which, at the moment, we have very few of.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:08.780
Which is why I am obsessed with this and I'm so proud to be a part of it and I want to say to

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:16.400
everybody who has watched, who has participated over the last ten years, thank you for being my leader.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:23.460
Also what I'm saying is, this video is longer than four minutes, isn't it? And I couldn't do anything about that.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:27.780
Not made a lot of overtime videos. I'm doing it right now because I just couldn't.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:33.960
And also I had at the end I wanted to say that this community is getting together in the real world

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:41.940
for our first, like, this is a central place that we're trying to get as many Nerdfighters in the same place at the same time for

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:47.140
in Boston, February 25th and 26th at NerdCon: Nerdfighteria and I hope very much that

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:55.100
as many people who can come will come because I know that being in the real world with people is

00:05:55.100 --> 00:06:00.500
an important part of community and it's an important part of me learning about this community

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:01.820
and being led by it.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:07.540
And also since those two things, they mesh together so well, this long video and NerdCon: Nerdfighteria

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:13.820
I have a great chance, an opportunity if you will, to get punished in front of people.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:19.380
So I've decided that I'm gonna do that. So this video is too long, punishment coming.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.400
We will record it and upload it.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:28.660
But the people at NerdCon: Nerdfighteria will get to see it live. Not sure what it's going to be yet, but I will be punished.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:32.420
It's been a while and I'm looking forward to seeing some folks in February.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:33.940
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

